# boundary_walk
You go down the slope by the goat path, with the cloak's hood over your head and your hands in your sleeves, to the fence line you have walked every week of the life you built.
It is all here. The posts, the barley stubble, the ditch where the water stands in spring. It is all here and none of it is yours any longer, because you have not walked here for four days, and in those four days it has stopped being land and become a place where someone is dead.
$ do act(1, true)
~> relic [role]
The sun is a hand on your back. {cap(voice("hunger"))}.
?if b1.goat == "freed"
  At the far corner a boy of twelve or so is sitting on the fence with a goat on a rope, scratching its ears with the absentminded, total attention of a child who has been given something back and does not yet believe it. He looks up. He sees a hooded figure at the end of the field. He does not run. He stares at you for a very long time, and then he lifts one hand, and goes on scratching the goat.
  $ do meet("quintus")
  $ bond.quintus += 5
?else
  Farther along, a boy of twelve or so is mending a fence with a face that does not match his hands. It is the boy from the square. He is not looking at the hill. He is, you think, very carefully not looking at the hill.
  $ do meet("quintus")
?end
<~

# relic
You stand for a moment at the corner post, with the line of your own land running away from you. It is your land, and you are dead. It will be ordinary to say so, to yourself, in a day or two.
<~

# relic_gladiator
Leaning against the post is the wooden practice sword, the one Aelia fights the bean rows with. She has left it where she was called in. You pick it up, and your hands know the weight of it, and you put it back exactly where she left it.
<~

# relic_legionary
The cloth wrapped sword is not here. It is in the roof beam, where it has always been, and you stand a long time with your eyes on the house and your hand on the empty place at your hip, and think of a horn that no one else can hear.
<~

# relic_merchant
Your whole body thinks of the ledger under the bed. You are already doing the sums: the barley, the wine, the debts that were owed to you, and who will remember them, and who will not. You are dead, and your accounts are open.
<~

# relic_physician_aide
Along the fence the herbs you planted are in flower, the yarrow and the little blue plant the old women swear by. There are four of them to pull for a poultice and you stand and look at them, and you cannot make your hands go down to them.
<~

# relic_house_slave
You pass through the field without disturbing a stalk, the way you once crossed a room in a house that was not yours. Nobody sees you. It is your oldest skill, and you notice for the first time that it was never really a skill. It was only fear, put to use.
<~

# ridge_watch
There is a ledge behind the villa where the hill falls away, and from it the whole of Collesanto lies open under the sun like a handful of tiles.
$ do act(1, true)
You can see the square, the fountain, the tree. You can see the black of the women at the well and the empty bier that someone has set outside your door because nobody knows what else to do with it. You can see the smoke of a dozen hearths. It is a village in grief, and it is going about its work, and it is the loveliest thing you have ever seen.
Then you see the others.
They are on the north road, where it comes out of the oak wood, a column of eleven men in plain grey cloaks, with no banners and no armor. There is a mule train behind them, loaded with long bundles and wicker baskets that clink. They are not soldiers, and they are not pilgrims, and they are not traders. They look like men who have come to do one thing and have prayed over it.
They halt beyond the stream, in the shade of the wood, and make no fire in the ordinary way. One of them kneels and sets a small clay lamp on a flat stone. The flame in it does not flicker. He tends it the way a priest tends an altar.
Something in you goes very still. It is the part of you that has never been able to leave an unfair thing alone. It is telling you what the lamp is for.
:the_strangers
* Follow them, unseen
  $ b1.warning = "followed"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 3
  $ do act(1, true)
  You go down through the wood by a way no living soul could have found, and you are close enough, by the end, to hear them. There is not much to hear. They do not talk. They kneel in rows and murmur the name of Vesta, goddess of the hearth, the way people say a name when they mean something else by it. Their leader, a lean man in a grey hood, tells them that the beast on the hill is not yet awake. They will wait for a night without a moon. They will come from the north, and take the thatch first. The word they use for what they are hunting is one you have heard in nursery stories: strix.
  You go back up the hill with your skin crawling, and the sun feels like nothing at all.
* Go down and warn them
  $ b1.warning = "warned"
  $ do witness(1)
  There is a boy at the well. He is the only living person who will see you, and you choose him because he is twelve and nobody listens to him. You pull your hood forward and tell him, as quietly as you can, that men with torches are coming, from the north, in the dark, and that the elders must set a watch. He stares at you with wide eyes and asks who you are, and you do not answer. You are gone before he can ask again.
  $ do meet("quintus")
* Say nothing
  $ b1.warning = "none"
  You cannot be sure of what you saw. You tell yourself that. You tell yourself that you are not the kind of person who would be believed, even by yourself. It is a very Roman excuse, and you do not like how well it fits.
<~

# dusk
## Dusk
$ do rest()
The sun goes down in the long gold way of the end of harvest, and you find you are sorry to see it go. You have not been so tired since the fever.
$ do discover("the_hunger")
The villa grows dark around you. When it is dark enough that you can no longer see the painted hunt above your head, you pull the cloak around your shoulders and walk out through the door and down the long hill toward the lights of the village, as you have walked it a thousand times, and as you will never walk it again.
=> next
